From: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] AP MLD: suggest a preferred TID-to-link mapping

hostapd only accepts or denies a TID-to-link mapping negotiation, and
suggests nothing but the default mapping, so an AP MLD cannot steer a
non-AP MLD to the mapping it prefers.

Add hostapd_neg_ttlm_policy_set(), which takes a switch for the
negotiation and up to 32 preferred mappings of the AP MLD, each for one
non-AP MLD or, with the broadcast address, for every non-AP MLD without
one of its own. A preferred mapping is used only where the AP MLD would
accept it as a request of the non-AP MLD.

- A TID-To-Link Mapping Request frame that differs from the preferred
  mapping gets status 134 with it in the Response frame
  (IEEE Std 802.11be-2024, 35.3.7.2.3); one equal to it is accepted.
- A mapping of a (Re)Association Request frame that differs from it is
  rejected. The Response frame carries the preferred mapping in place
  of the default mapping, and the association stands (NOTE 1 of
  35.3.7.2.3). Once the station is authorized, an unsolicited
  TID-To-Link Mapping Response frame with Dialog Token 0 and status 134
  carries it again (35.3.7.2.3, 9.6.38.3). A Protected EHT Action frame
  needs the keys of the station.
- While an established advertised mapping is in the Response frame,
  that element answers the request, as before.
- With the negotiation off, the AP MLD advertises support 0, tears down
  each negotiated mapping, and the default mapping applies (35.3.7.2.2).

A new preferred mapping keeps a negotiated mapping: it applies to the
negotiations after it, and a negotiated mapping holds until a teardown
or a new negotiation (35.3.7.2.3).

Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>

